    <section class="section">
        <div class="container">
            <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
            <p class="sec-sub">The real questions operators ask us before signing.</p>
            <div style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto;">
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">How is freight-forwarding marketing different from generic B2B marketing?</h3><div class="faq-a">Sales cycles are 3-9 months and RFP-gated. Buyers are Head of Supply Chain + procurement + import/export managers who search for route + tariff + capacity content before vendor content. Channel mix skews to LinkedIn ABM (45%) + Google Search (25%) + industry PR (20%) + email nurture (10%) rather than Meta + display. Content must be technically credible &mdash; AI-only content damages authority signal.</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">Which trade publications should a freight forwarder be in?</h3><div class="faq-a">For India + South Asia: Cargo Talk, Logistics Insider, Maritime Gateway, ITLN, STAT Times. For international: JOC (Journal of Commerce), Lloyd's List, The Loadstar, Air Cargo News. Placement strategies vary &mdash; sponsored data reports vs earned editorial vs speaker slots at conferences. We manage relationships with all of these + place 6-10 pieces per year for Growth-tier clients.</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">What is a realistic LinkedIn ABM ROI for a freight forwarder?</h3><div class="faq-a">150-300 account TAL typically produces 30-60 accepted connections + 8-15 InMail replies + 3-8 qualified opportunities over a 12-week cycle. At Rs 8-25 lakh average deal size (mid-market forwarders) with 20-30% close rate on qualified opportunities, ABM ROI is 3-8x in year 1 and compounds materially in year 2+ once the TAL is refined.</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">Do we need gated whitepapers for freight forwarding marketing?</h3><div class="faq-a">Yes for enterprise B2B. Gated content is the primary lead-magnet for freight forwarders because buyers actively search for route + tariff + market-outlook content. A single well-designed whitepaper can generate 40-120 SQLs over 6-12 months with proper amplification (LinkedIn Ads + trade-publication placement + partner cross-promotion).</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">What CPL should we expect for freight forwarding leads?</h3><div class="faq-a">Google Search Ads Rs 1,500-4,500 per raw lead. LinkedIn ABM Rs 3,500-9,000 per MQL. Gated whitepaper downloads Rs 400-1,200 per lead but require nurture. Trade-publication earned editorial has no direct CPL but drives significant brand-search lift + inbound TAL account engagement (indirect ROI).</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">Should freight forwarders run Meta or Instagram ads?</h3><div class="faq-a">Generally no for enterprise B2B. Yes if you have a cross-border e-com forwarding line targeting D2C brand founders scaling internationally &mdash; Meta + WhatsApp reach D2C founder personas cost-effectively. Split the funnel: LinkedIn + Google + PR for enterprise; Meta + WhatsApp for D2C-facing cross-border.</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">How long is a typical freight forwarding sales cycle?</h3><div class="faq-a">3-9 months from first LinkedIn touch to signed shipping contract for mid-market. 6-18 months for enterprise (tender + RFP-gated). Marketing programmes must budget for these cycles &mdash; "90-day pipeline" expectations are unrealistic. Pipeline coverage of 3-4x quota is what to target.</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">Can we run LinkedIn ABM in-house or do we need an agency?</h3><div class="faq-a">In-house works for very small (25-50 account) programmes founder-led. Beyond that, an agency partnership makes sense because ABM requires specialist skills (Sales Nav filtering, InMail creative, cadence management, retargeting + LinkedIn Ads, gated content production, CRM integration) that most freight forwarders do not have in-house. Hybrid model (in-house senior + agency execution) is what most mid-market forwarders end up on.</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">Do you help with FIATA / WCA / ACAAI member co-marketing?</h3><div class="faq-a">Yes. Partner-network amplification (joint case studies, cross-network lead pooling, network-branded gated content, member-directory optimisation) is a mid-tier opportunity for network-member forwarders. Different playbook from direct-to-shipper marketing but generates meaningful inbound from partner referrals.</div></div>
            <div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-q">What is the biggest mistake freight forwarders make with marketing?</h3><div class="faq-a">Investing in Google Display + retargeting + generic content before building LinkedIn ABM + gated content library + industry PR. Enterprise B2B logistics buyers are a small, sophisticated, hard-to-reach audience. Broad-reach digital tactics burn budget without moving the pipeline. ABM-first, content-second, everything else third.</div></div>
            </div>
